diagnosis_text:
A complete five-layer diagnostic found no fault anywhere in the topology at the time of investigation. Phase 1 ICMP reachability showed 0% loss across every tested path. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed clean ACL tables, correct addressing on both hosts, no static ARP poisoning, no resolver divergence, and no duplicate MAC addresses across all 15 devices. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed that all six FRR routers are fully operational — every OSPF adjacency is in Full state, all daemons are active and reachable via vtysh, and no traffic-control shaping exists on any interface. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) is the most directly relevant: from host_1_1_1_1 itself, DNS correctly resolves web2.local to 10.200.0.5 and web3.local to 10.200.0.6; HTTP GETs to both URLs return HTTP 200 in approximately 4 ms; both web_server_2 and web_server_3 answer localhost HTTP with 200 OK; and no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion appear on any device. There is no flap script, no carrier-error history, no daemon crash log, and no mechanism that would produce the intermittent or transient failure described in the ticket. The user's reported symptom — web2.local and web3.local not loading from host_1_1_1_1 — is not reproducible and leaves no trace. Root cause: no fault is present; the symptom either resolved before investigation began or was a client-side transient (browser cache, proxy, or local OS resolver) unrelated to the lab network. Fix: no network-side corrective action is required; advise the user to clear browser cache and retry.
